---
/** Why, cases 3 and 4: what a screen does about focus, and what it costs while it is closed. */
import Case from './Case.astro';
import Live from './Live.astro';
import BridgeLog from './BridgeLog.astro';
import Code from '../Code.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';
import * as sample from '../../samples/why-screens';
---

<Case
  id="why-focus"
  index={3}
  title="Focus, the cursor and Escape are rewritten in every resource"
  todayEnds="Every resource keeps this bookkeeping, and one missed case is a stuck cursor."
  nexusEnds="One tag. Focus follows the screens that are open, and is released when the resource stops."
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    A screen declares in its own file what it takes from the player and how it closes. The build writes that down for Lua, and the
    client runtime gives and releases focus as screens open and close.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="today">
    <Code code={sample.focusLua} lang="lua" file="client/main.lua" tone="bad" lines={{ 5: 'bad', 11: 'bad', 19: 'bad' }} />
    <Code code={sample.focusPage} lang="js" file="web/app.js" tone="bad" />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="nexus">
    <Code code={sample.screenTag} lang="nexus" file="web/screens/Shop.nexus" tone="good" lines={{ 1: 'good' }} />
    <Code code={sample.screenLua} lang="lua" file="client/main.lua" tone="good" />
    <Code code={sample.screensLua} lang="lua" file="nexus/screens.lua" note="generated" tone="good" />
    <ul class="case-facts">
      <li>The screen opened last that asks for focus has it. When it closes, focus goes back to the one before, or to the game.</li>
      <li>Escape closes one screen at a time, and the pause menu is kept shut for a quarter of a second after it.</li>
      <li>A screen on the <code class="ic">hud</code> layer never takes focus.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</Case>

<Case
  id="why-idle"
  index={4}
  title="A closed UI still costs"
  todayEnds="A parked car still sends ten messages a second, and a closed menu still holds its framework."
  nexusEnds="A parked car sends nothing. With nothing open, the page has no nodes and runs nothing."
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    A screen's code is loaded when it is first opened, and its nodes are removed when it closes. State is patched, not resent:
    <code class="ic">Nexus.set</code> compares with what it sent before and sends only the keys that changed.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="today">
    <Code code={sample.idleLua} lang="lua" file="client/main.lua" tone="bad" lines={{ 4: 'bad' }} />
    <Code code={sample.idlePage} lang="jsx" file="web/main.jsx" tone="bad" />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="nexus">
    <Code code={sample.setLua} lang="lua" file="client/main.lua" tone="good" lines={{ 6: 'good' }} />
    <Code code={sample.hudScreen} lang="nexus" file="web/screens/Hud.nexus" tone="good" />
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="result">
    <BridgeLog lines={sample.stateLog} caption="What crosses the bridge for a moving car" />
    <Live example="hud">A HUD fed by state, which the mock changes on a timer as the game would.</Live>
    <p class="case-note">
      Each line is a patch with the keys that changed, never the whole state. In the page, only the node that shows a changed key
      updates. The details are in <a href={href('/docs/guide/#7-a-hud-fed-by-lua')}>the guide</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</Case>
